# The messages of the kernel (`tauto-kernel`): the diagnostics of checking
# declarations, and why a declaration is skipped. Names, terms and types
# are variables, written as they are.

kernel-core-type-mismatch = type mismatch
kernel-core-unknown-atom = an unknown atom
kernel-core-not-function = an application of something that is not a function
kernel-core-unexpected-function = a function where a function type is not expected
kernel-core-not-type = not a type
kernel-core-underscore-not-value = `_` is not a value
kernel-core-not-defined = `{ $name }` is not defined in the core
kernel-core-numeral-too-large = the numeral `{ $numeral }` is too large
kernel-core-not-kernel-term = not a kernel term
kernel-core-quot-has-type = a constant of the quotient has a type
kernel-core-quot-no-value = `{ $name }` is a constant of the quotient and has no value
kernel-core-quot-declared-after = `{ $name }` is declared after { $needs }
kernel-core-quot-type = `{ $name }` must have the type the core gives it
kernel-core-bvar-out-of-scope = a bound variable out of scope
kernel-core-level-overflow = universe level overflow
kernel-core-name-not-defined = a name not defined in the core
kernel-core-cannot-infer-function = cannot infer the type of a function
kernel-core-cannot-type-applied = cannot type an applied function
kernel-core-already-defined = `{ $name }` is already defined in the core
kernel-core-group-share-params = the families of a group share their parameters
kernel-core-family-fewer-params = a family has fewer parameters than declared
kernel-core-group-sort = the families of a group live in the declared sort
kernel-core-ctor-already-defined = `{ $name }` is already defined
kernel-core-ctor-take-params = `{ $name }` must take the parameters
kernel-core-ctor-arg-universe = an argument of `{ $name }` lives in a bigger universe than its family
kernel-core-ctor-must-return = `{ $name }` must return `{ $family }` applied to its parameters
kernel-core-index-mentions-family = an index of `{ $name }` mentions its family
kernel-core-nested-not-inductive = a nested type is not inductive
kernel-core-nested-ctor-params = a nested constructor takes its type's parameters
kernel-core-nested-only-type = nested occurrences are only supported in `Type`
kernel-core-eliminator-not-closed = an eliminator's type is not closed
kernel-core-nested-no-params = a nested occurrence is only supported in a type without parameters
kernel-core-cannot-nest = `{ $name }` cannot be nested: it must be a plain type with parameters
kernel-core-nested-depends = a nested occurrence must not depend on a variable
kernel-core-recursive-arg-params = `{ $name }` must be applied to its parameters in a recursive argument
kernel-core-negative = a family of the group occurs in a negative position
kernel-core-nested-or-negative = a family of the group occurs in a nested or negative position
kernel-core-recursor-universe = recursor universe exceeds 65535
kernel-core-unknown-family = unknown inductive family
kernel-core-no-large-elimination = the family does not permit large elimination
kernel-core-missing-recursor = missing recursor
kernel-core-invalid-telescope = invalid recursor telescope
kernel-core-invalid-motive = invalid recursor motive
kernel-uses-failed-then = uses `{ $name }`, which did not check; what follows may come from that: { $message }
kernel-uses-failed = uses `{ $name }`, which did not check
kernel-gave-up = gave up after { $seconds }s of checking
kernel-gave-up-unfolded = gave up after { $seconds }s of checking (unfolded most: { $top })
kernel-unfolded-times = `{ $name }` { $count } times
kernel-out-of-steps = gave up after { $steps } steps of reduction
kernel-out-of-steps-in = gave up after { $steps } steps of reduction, unfolding `{ $name }` (unfolded most: { $top }); raise the limit of the declaration with `budget`
kernel-file-time-limit = checking { $file } took more than { $seconds }s and was stopped; to allow it more, list it under check.slow in Tauto.eure with a larger limit
kernel-ran-out-of-time = not checked: `{ $name }` ran out of time
kernel-past-u128 =
    { $message }
    note: `{ $op }` is past u128 and was left as it is: the kernel does not compute closed numbers that large
kernel-handle-not-read = its `handle` is not read in proofs: { $why }
kernel-reserved = `{ $name }` is reserved and cannot name a declaration
kernel-already-defined = `{ $name }` is already defined
kernel-mutual-empty = a `mutual` group needs at least one declaration
kernel-mutual-decidable = `{ $name }` lists a decidable equality, and a type in a mutual group has none
kernel-mutual-mixed = a mutual block holds only inductive families, or only functions
kernel-mutual-plain-generics = `{ $name }` is not defined by a match, so its calls in the block are written out, and a function written out has no generics
kernel-mutual-plain-cycle = `{ $name }` reaches itself through functions not defined by a match: a recursion in a mutual block goes through a function defined by a match
kernel-mutual-plain-partial = `{ $name }` is not defined by a match, so the block writes out its calls, and `{ $user }` uses it without all its arguments
kernel-mutual-plain-too-large = writing out the calls of the functions not defined by a match would make the block { $growth } times as large: `{ $name }` would be copied { $times } times. Pass the results of the recursive calls to `{ $name }` as arguments, so that it no longer calls the block and is not written out
kernel-mutual-result-type = a function of a mutual block has a result type
kernel-mutual-param-typed = a parameter of a mutual block is typed
kernel-mutual-no-effects = a function of a mutual block has no effects; a function with effects is executable code, and needs no `mutual` to call the others
kernel-mutual-theorem-proof = a theorem of a mutual block has a proof
kernel-mutual-hole = a hole in a `mutual` block is not supported
kernel-uses-skipped = uses `{ $name }`, which is not one of the kernel's
kernel-uses-skipped-why = `{ $name }` is not defined in proofs: it is declared earlier, but the kernel did not check it: { $why }; a proof uses only what the kernel checked
kernel-has-effects = has effects; not usable in proofs: { $why }
kernel-result-effect-row = its result type holds a function with effects; not usable in proofs
kernel-param-effect-row = its parameter `{ $name }` holds a function with effects; not usable in proofs
kernel-no-result-type = no result type
kernel-untyped-param = untyped parameter
kernel-body-executable = body is executable code, not a kernel term
kernel-type-not-kernel = its type is not one of the kernel's
kernel-not-kernel-term-match = not a kernel term: match. A `match` is not written here: define a `fn` whose body is the match and call it
kernel-not-kernel-term-text = not a kernel term: text (a text literal is read as a kernel term only after `Text` is declared; add `import std::Text`)
kernel-not-kernel-term-kind = not a kernel term: { $kind }
kernel-block-value = a block in a proof ends with its value
kernel-block-lets = a block in a proof holds only `let x: T := e` before its value
kernel-statement-discarded = the value of this statement is not used, so its type would never be checked
kernel-let-unused = `{ $name }` is defined but not used
kernel-mismatch = type mismatch: expected `{ $expected }`, found `{ $found }`
kernel-mismatch-line =
    { $message }
    {"  "}{ $line }
kernel-mismatch-differing = differing part: expected `{ $expected }`, found `{ $found }`
kernel-side-expected = expected
kernel-side-found = found
kernel-mismatch-computed = the { $side } `{ $value }` is computed from `{ $call }`
kernel-mismatch-expected-written = expected is written as { $written }
kernel-mismatch-found-written = found is written as { $written }
kernel-hole-twice = the hole `?{ $name }` is written twice
kernel-underscore-not-value = `_` is not a value
kernel-float-not-defined = `Float` is not defined in proofs until it is declared; add `import std::Float`
kernel-integer-division = `/` is the division of fractions, and the proofs do not define it on integers; write `Nat::div(a, b)`, `Nat::rem(a, b)`, `Int::tdiv`, `Int::tmod`, `Int::fdiv` or `Int::fmod` (and `U8::div`, `I32::tdiv`, … for the fixed widths), and `Rat::div` for fractions
kernel-unit-value-as-type = `#()` is the unit value, not a type; write the unit type as `Unit`
kernel-calls-itself = `{ $name }` calls itself outside a structural match; there is no other termination check
kernel-not-defined = `{ $name }` is not defined
kernel-not-defined-hint = `{ $name }` is not defined; did you mean the constructor `{ $hint }`?
kernel-not-defined-yet = `{ $name }` is not defined yet: it is declared further on, and it uses this declaration, directly or through others; declarations that use each other are written together in a `mutual` block
kernel-not-defined-struct = `{ $name }` is not defined in proofs: a `struct` or an `enum` is not one of the kernel's types; write `inductive` before it (`inductive struct`, `inductive enum`) to use it here
kernel-hole-type-unknown = the type of the hole `?{ $name }` is not known here: it needs an expected type
kernel-not-kernel-term = not a kernel term
kernel-cannot-infer-function = cannot infer the type of a function here; it needs an expected type
kernel-cannot-type-applied = cannot type an applied function here
kernel-too-many-arguments = `{ $name }` is applied to too many arguments
kernel-function-given = a function was given where `{ $expected }` is expected
kernel-not-a-type = `{ $term }` is not a type
kernel-core-rejects = the core kernel rejects `{ $name }`: { $message }
kernel-sort-names-variable = `{ $name }` is a sort and cannot name a variable
kernel-binder-one-name = a binder of the kernel binds one name
kernel-universe-name-taken = `{ $name }` is reserved or already declared; universe parameters do not create a separate source namespace
kernel-universe-no-deq = generated equality declarations on universe-polymorphic inductives are not supported
kernel-universe-takes-levels = `{ $name }` takes { $levels } level(s) before its arguments
kernel-universe-level-numeral = a level argument is a numeral expression (`0`, `1 + 1`, `max(1, 2)`); levels are not inferred
kernel-universe-level-expression = a level argument is a numeral expression or one over the template's level parameters (`u + 1`, `max(u, v)`); levels are not inferred
kernel-universe-match-ctor = matching universe-polymorphic constructors is not supported; use the explicit family eliminator with its universe levels
kernel-universe-write-levels = `{ $name }` is level-polymorphic: write its levels, `{ $name }(0, …)`
kernel-universe-budget = universe specialization budget exceeded (128 nested instances, 4096 total instances)
kernel-universe-level-range = universe specialization requires a numeral level expression within 0..65535
kernel-recursor-arguments = recursor takes a family name and an explicit universe level
kernel-recursor-family = recursor expects an unapplied inductive family (specialize its universe levels first)
kernel-recursor-level-numeral = recursor universe is a numeral expression; levels are not inferred
kernel-recursor-level-expression = recursor universe is a numeral expression or one over the template's level parameters; levels are not inferred
kernel-recursor-not-family = `{ $name }` is not a checked inductive family
kernel-recursor-no-type = `{ $name }` does not permit elimination into Type
kernel-universe-template-invalid = universe templates cannot shadow a level parameter or contain unsupported nested declarations or unexpanded binding syntax
kernel-deq-not-inductive = it is not an inductive
kernel-deq-param-not-type = a parameter is not a type
kernel-deq-indices = it is a family with indices
kernel-deq-mutual = it is in a mutual group
kernel-deq-alias = it is an alias
kernel-deq-no-ctors = it has no constructors
kernel-deq-proposition = it is a proposition
kernel-deq-not-declared = `{ $name }` is not declared
kernel-deq-field = the field `{ $field }` of `{ $ctor }` has type `{ $ty }`, which has no decidable equality (a type that has one lists `fn T::beq, theorem T::beq_refl, theorem T::beq_sound, theorem T::deq` after its sort)
kernel-deq-no-parse = the generated text does not parse
kernel-deq-no-check = the generated declarations do not check
kernel-deq-not-generated = it was not generated
kernel-deq-cannot-make = `{ $name }` lists a decidable equality that cannot be made: { $why }
kernel-deq-list-mismatch = `{ $name }`: the list after its sort does not match what an inductive can make besides its eliminators (the four of a decidable equality)
kernel-match-the-matched-value = the matched value
kernel-match-the-parameter = the parameter `{ $name }`
kernel-match-arm-hides = the arm `{ $arm }` names a variable `{ $name }`, which is also the name of { $what }; rename the variable
kernel-match-mutual-by-match = a function of a mutual block must be defined by a match
kernel-match-on-parameter = a match must be on a parameter
kernel-match-as-variable = `eqn` names the equation of a match on a term; a match on a variable has the variable itself
kernel-match-not-variable = `{ $name }` is not a variable in scope that a match can eliminate
kernel-match-lambda-type = the type of `{ $name }` is not known here: a λ's parameter has the type the called function gives it, once the arguments outside a `match` fix its generics, or the type written on it: `({ $name }: T) => …`
kernel-match-not-inductive = the matched value is not of an inductive type
kernel-match-quot = a quotient cannot be matched: a match could tell related values apart. Use `Quot::lift` (with a proof that the function gives related values the same result) or `Quot::ind`
kernel-match-family-not-inductive = `{ $name }` is not an inductive type
kernel-match-one-group = the functions of a mutual block match on families of one group
kernel-match-proofs-or-data = the functions of a block are all proofs or all data
kernel-match-no-large-elimination = `{ $name }` cannot be eliminated into data: it is a proposition without large elimination
kernel-match-guards = guards are not supported in kernel matches
kernel-match-named-last = the arm `{ $name } =>`, which matches every value, must be the last
kernel-match-default-last = the default arm `_ =>` must be the last
kernel-match-named-indices = an arm `y =>` that names the matched value needs the matched family's indices to be variables; write `_ =>` and use the matched variable
kernel-match-lists-ctors = a kernel match lists constructors (nested and variable patterns are not supported)
kernel-match-not-ctor = `{ $name }` is not a constructor
kernel-match-not-ctor-hint = `{ $name }` is not a constructor; did you mean `{ $hint }`?
kernel-match-other-ctor = `{ $name }` is a constructor of `{ $family }`, not `{ $matched }`
kernel-match-other-ctor-alone = `{ $name }` is a constructor of `{ $family }`, not `{ $matched }`; a name in a pattern that is a constructor matches that constructor, so to bind the value write a name that is not one, like `{ $binder }`
kernel-match-field-pattern = a field's pattern is a name or a constructor
kernel-match-field-count = `{ $name }` has { $fields } fields, the pattern has { $written }
kernel-match-twice = `{ $name }` is matched twice
kernel-match-named-unused = the arm `{ $name } =>` matches no constructor: the other arms name them all
kernel-match-default-unused = the default arm `_ =>` matches no constructor
kernel-match-same-family-indices = two functions of a block match on the same family, whose indices are not variables; this is not supported
kernel-match-missing-case = missing case `{ $name }`
kernel-match-same-family-function = two functions of a block match on the same family, which has a function-typed field; this is not supported
kernel-match-not-compiled = a match could not be compiled
kernel-match-result-type = the type of this match is not known here, so it needs its result type: `⊢ T from match x {"{"} … {"}"}` (it is known as a declaration's body, an arm's body, a λ's body with a known type, and an argument of a call whose other arguments fix the parameter's type)
kernel-match-param-written = the parameter `{ $name }` is written as `{ $written }`, but here it is a `{ $given }`
kernel-field-not-inductive = `.` is on a value of an inductive type
kernel-field-many-ctors = `{ $family }` has more than one constructor: `.{ $name }` is a match
kernel-field-of-type = `{ $family }` is a type, and a field is read from a value: for `x` of type `{ $family }`, write `x.{ $name }`
kernel-field-none = `{ $family }` has no field `{ $name }`
kernel-field-depends = the type of `{ $name }` depends on another field
kernel-ascribe-mismatch = `{ $have }` is not `{ $want }`, nor a product with its members
kernel-ascribe-no-member = `{ $product }` has no member of the type `{ $member }`
kernel-ascribe-clash = two members of a product have the type `{ $member }`, so the members cannot be matched by their types; write the members by position, or tell them apart with a label (`@x { $member }`)
kernel-ascribe-open = `{ $have }` and `{ $want }` are matched by the types of their members only where neither mentions a type variable; write the members in the same order, by position, or give each a label (`@x T`)
kernel-eq-no-deq = `==` is on `Nat`, `Bool`, `Text` or a type with a decidable equality, not `{ $ty }`; an inductive has one when it lists `=> fn { $head }::beq, theorem { $head }::beq_refl, theorem { $head }::beq_sound, theorem { $head }::deq` after its sort
kernel-eq-type-open = Neither side of `=` has a type to take: a numeric literal, `[]`, a hole, a λ, or a constructor with parameters written without arguments (`Nil`, `None`) leaves it open. Write `Eq(T, a, b)`
kernel-if-type-open = Neither branch of this `if` has a type to take: a numeric literal, `[]`, a hole, a λ, or a constructor with parameters written without arguments (`Nil`, `None`) leaves it open. Write its type: `⊢ T from match c {"{"} true => a  false => b {"}"}`
kernel-if-no-else = an `if` in a kernel term needs an `else` branch
kernel-eq-not-declared = `==` here is `{ $name }`, which is not declared
kernel-match-arm-goal = the goal of this arm is `{ $computed }`, not `{ $written }`
kernel-rec-without-call = `{ $name }` is used without a call; recursion must be a call on a field of the matched value
kernel-rec-arity = `{ $name }` is not structural: a recursive call passes every argument ({ $given } here, { $expected } expected)
kernel-rec-not-smaller = `{ $name }` is not structural: `{ $function }` matches on its parameter `{ $param }`, so the argument for `{ $param }` is a part of the value matched here (a field, a field of a field matched in the arm, a function-typed field applied to all its arguments, or a part matched in the arm written as the arm's constructor), and `{ $argument }` is not. { $hint }
kernel-rec-not-smaller-group = `{ $name }` is not structural: `{ $function }` matches on its parameter `{ $param }`, so the argument for `{ $param }` is a part of the value matched here (a field, a field of a field matched in the arm, a function-typed field applied to all its arguments, or a part matched in the arm written as the arm's constructor), and `{ $argument }` is not. `{ $part }` is a part of another type of the group, and a recursion through its parts is a function that matches on that type, together with this one in one `mutual {"{"} … {"}"}` block. { $hint }
kernel-rec-hint-swapped = `{ $part }` is a part of `{ $param }`, but this call passes it for `{ $other }`; pass it for `{ $param }`, as in `{ $suggestion }` (the arguments are in the order of the parameters)
kernel-rec-hint-stuck = Every argument of this call is the parameter's own, so the call does not get smaller; give it an argument that is a part of `{ $param }`
kernel-rec-hint-part = Pass a variable bound by a pattern of the `match` on `{ $param }` (a field of the value) for `{ $param }`
kernel-rec-hint-by-one = If the recursion is on `{ $param }`, which this call changes, name it after the result type: `decreasing_by { $param }`, and match on it in the body (a well-founded measure is `decreasing_by measure(f)`, on the first parameter)
kernel-rec-hint-by-many = If the recursion is on a parameter this call changes ({ $params }), name it after the result type: `decreasing_by { $first }`, and match on it in the body (a well-founded measure is `decreasing_by measure(f)`, on the first parameter)
kernel-rec-changes-later = `{ $name }` changes a later parameter
kernel-rec-changes-earlier = `{ $name }` changes an earlier parameter
kernel-rec-fixed-one = `{ $name }` is not structural: the recursion is on `{ $param }`, the matched parameter, and the type of `{ $param }` depends on { $fixed }, so a recursive call passes it unchanged
kernel-rec-fixed-many = `{ $name }` is not structural: the recursion is on `{ $param }`, the matched parameter, and the type of `{ $param }` depends on { $fixed }, so a recursive call passes them unchanged
kernel-decreasing-structural = `decreasing_by` on a structural recursion names a parameter; a well-founded relation is for a `fn`
kernel-decreasing-not-param = `decreasing_by { $name }`: `{ $name }` is not a parameter
kernel-decreasing-not-inductive = `decreasing_by { $name }`: `{ $name }` is not of an inductive type
kernel-rec-deep-elimination = a recursion on a part deeper than a field of `{ $name }` needs `{ $family }` to eliminate into types
kernel-rec-deep-result = a recursion on a part deeper than a field needs a result in `Type` or `Prop`
kernel-match-no-equation-binder = internal: the motive has no equation binder
kernel-unify-depends = the index fixes `{ $name }` to a term that mentions `{ $mentioned }`, whose type depends on it; this unification is not supported
kernel-unify-different-types = the indices belong to different types
kernel-unify-no-confusion = `{ $name }` has no no-confusion, so its values cannot be unified
kernel-unify-index = cannot unify the index `{ $left }` with `{ $right }` in the match on `{ $family }`
kernel-unify-cyclic = the indices determine variables in terms of each other; this unification is not supported
kernel-class-skipped = not a class of the proof language: { $why }
kernel-class-one-param = it has one parameter of kind `Type`
kernel-class-defined = class `{ $name }` is already defined
kernel-class-method-effects = `{ $name }` performs effects
kernel-class-effects-laws = { $class }::{ $method } performs effects, so { $class } cannot have laws
kernel-class-method-generics = `{ $name }` has generics
kernel-class-method-untyped = `{ $name }` has an untyped parameter
kernel-instance-not-class-skipped = `{ $name }` is not a class of the proof language
kernel-instance-head = an instance of `{ $name }` is for a type with a name at its head
kernel-instance-defined = instance { $instance } is already defined
kernel-instance-not-class = `{ $name }` is not a class
kernel-instance-lacks = instance { $instance } lacks the method `{ $method }`
kernel-instance-method-params = the method `{ $method }` of { $instance } has { $written } parameters, but the class has { $class }
kernel-instance-not-method = `{ $name }` is not a method of { $class }
kernel-instance-not-law = `{ $name }` is not a law of { $class }
kernel-instance-law-params = the law { $law } of { $instance } has different parameters from the law of the class
kernel-instance-law-differs = the law { $law } of { $instance } states a different proposition
kernel-effect-types = the effect's types are not the kernel's: { $why }
kernel-effect-reading-fails = has effects; its reading in proofs does not check: { $why }
kernel-effect-row-variable = its row variable `{ $name }` is not one of its generics
kernel-effect-row-variables = its row has more than one variable
kernel-effect-unread = its effect `{ $name }` is not one read in proofs
kernel-effect-no-result = it has no result type
kernel-effect-untyped-param = a parameter has no type
kernel-effect-where = it has a `where`
kernel-effect-bind-first = { $what } has effects; bind it with `let` first
kernel-effect-called-function = a called function
kernel-effect-if-condition = the condition of an `if`
kernel-effect-guard = a `match` arm with a guard has effects
kernel-effect-body-form = its body has a form with effects that is not read in proofs
kernel-effect-not-in-row = it performs `{ $name }`, which is not in its row
kernel-effect-arity = `{ $name }` takes another number of arguments
kernel-effect-call-not-in-row = `{ $function }` performs `{ $name }`, which is not in the row
kernel-effect-block-value = a block with effects ends with its value
kernel-effect-assign-field = `{ $place } :=` assigns a field, which is not read in proofs yet
kernel-effect-assign-not-var = `{ $name }` is assigned but is not a `var` of the function
kernel-effect-block-statement = a block with effects holds a statement not read in proofs
kernel-effect-var-type = write the type of `var { $name }`
kernel-effect-block-type = write the type of the value of the block that has `var { $name }`
kernel-effect-return-pattern = a `return` arm with a pattern
kernel-effect-handler-guard = a handler arm with a guard
kernel-effect-not-read = `{ $name }` is not an effect read in proofs
kernel-effect-handler-pattern = a handler arm with a pattern
kernel-effect-two-arms = two arms for `{ $name }`
kernel-effect-none-read = no effect is read in proofs
kernel-effect-param-row-variable = its parameter `{ $name }` is a function whose row has a variable its own row does not
kernel-effect-param-row-unread = its parameter `{ $name }` is a function whose row is not read in proofs: { $why }
kernel-effect-param-row-own = the row of its parameter `{ $name }` has the variable `{ $variable }`, which its own row does not
kernel-ctor-type-name = constructor `{ $name }` has the name of a type; types and constructors share one namespace, so name it otherwise (as `Mk{ $name }`)
kernel-ind-param-name = the parameter `{ $name }` of `{ $family }` needs a name of its own
kernel-ind-sort-index = `{ $name }` is a sort and cannot name an index
kernel-ind-ends-sort = an inductive family ends in a sort
kernel-ind-group-share = the families of a mutual group share their parameters and their sort
kernel-ind-sort-field = `{ $name }` is a sort and cannot name a field
kernel-ind-arg-universe = argument `{ $name }` of `{ $ctor }` lives in a bigger universe than `{ $family }`
kernel-ind-ctor-return = constructor `{ $ctor }` must return `{ $family }` applied to its parameters
kernel-ind-rec-params = `{ $family }` must be applied to its parameters `{ $params }` in the argument `{ $argument }` of `{ $ctor }`
kernel-ind-nested-type = nested occurrences are only supported in inductive types in `Type`
kernel-ind-negative = `{ $families }` occurs in a negative or nested position in `{ $term }`
kernel-implicit-nullary = `{ $name }` has no parameters and is named without `()`: write `{ $name }()` to call it
kernel-implicit-not-defined = `{ $name }` is not defined
kernel-implicit-fewer-params = `{ $name }` has fewer parameters than implicit ones
kernel-implicit-named-unknown = `{ $name }` has no implicit parameter `{ $field }`
kernel-implicit-named-twice = the implicit argument `{ $field }` is written twice
kernel-implicit-named-mismatch = the implicit argument `{ $field }` of `{ $name }` disagrees with what the call fixes
kernel-implicit-named-none = implicit arguments are written by name only in a call to a declaration with implicit parameters
kernel-implicit-named-partial = a call writing implicit arguments by name writes all { $count } explicit arguments of `{ $name }`
kernel-implicit-positional = `{ $name }` is given more arguments than its explicit parameters: implicit arguments are written by name, `{ $name }(<x: v>, …)`
kernel-implicit-no-match = cannot infer the implicit arguments of `{ $name }`: `{ $expected }` does not match `{ $found }`
kernel-implicit-cannot-infer = cannot infer all the implicit arguments of `{ $name }`
kernel-implicit-generic = cannot infer the generic `{ $generic }` of `{ $name }`: neither its arguments nor the expected type fix it; write the generics as its first arguments, `{ $name }({ $generics }, …)`
kernel-implicit-class-needed = a class is needed here
kernel-implicit-laws-of = the laws of { $instance }
kernel-implicit-class-type = cannot infer the type that `{ $class }` is needed for
kernel-implicit-no-instance = no instance of { $instance }
kernel-implicit-no-instance-ord = no instance of { $instance }; it is declared in std, add `import std::Ord`
kernel-implicit-instance-types = cannot infer all the types of the instance { $instance }
kernel-mut-field-family = `&mut { $name }.…` needs `{ $name }` typed by a family with one constructor
kernel-mut-lends = `&mut` lends a `var`, a `&mut` parameter or a field of one
kernel-mut-argument = `&mut` is an argument of a call
kernel-mut-lambda = a lambda does not change the state
kernel-mut-match-type = a `match` that changes the state needs its type: `let x: T := match …` or `⊢ T from match e …`
kernel-mut-var-type = `var { $name }` needs its type to be changed in a `match`: `var { $name }: T := …`
kernel-mut-term = this term does not change the state in a function in the proof language
kernel-mut-assign = `{ $name } :=` assigns a `var` or a `&mut` parameter
kernel-mut-block = a block in a function holds `let`, `var`, `:=` and terms
kernel-mut-param-type = `&mut { $name }` needs its type
kernel-mut-verdict = `{ $name }`: { $verdict }
kernel-resume-once = once
kernel-resume-times = { $count } times
kernel-resume-any = any number of times
kernel-resume-hint-once = declare it `resume <=1` to resume it
kernel-resume-hint-many = declare it `resume ω` to resume more than once
kernel-resume-overused = the arm for `{ $effect }` may call `{ $cont }` { $times }, but `{ $effect }` is declared `resume { $written }`; { $hint }
kernel-wf-list-mismatch = `decreasing_by`: the list of what `{ $name }` makes does not match what it made
kernel-wf-needs = `decreasing_by` needs `{ $name }`: import "WellFounded.taut"
kernel-wf-no-param = `decreasing_by` recurs on the first parameter, and there is none
kernel-wf-where = `decreasing_by` does not take `where` or effects
kernel-wf-result = `decreasing_by` needs a result type
kernel-wf-typed = `decreasing_by` needs every parameter typed
kernel-wf-call-arity = a recursive call of `{ $name }` takes its { $count } arguments and then the proof that the first is below `{ $param }`
kernel-wf-only-called = `{ $name }` is only called in its own body, with the proof
kernel-opaque-list-mismatch = `opaque fn`: the list of what `{ $name }` makes does not match what it made
kernel-opaque-wf = an `opaque fn` does not recur by `decreasing_by` on a relation; its recursion is structural
kernel-opaque-where = an `opaque fn` has no `where` and no effects
kernel-opaque-result = an `opaque fn` writes its result type
kernel-opaque-typed = an `opaque fn` writes the types of its parameters
kernel-nested-default-unused = the default arm `_ =>` matches no value: the other arms match them all
kernel-nested-overlap = these arms overlap: a field that one arm splits into constructors, every arm reaching it splits (only a last `_ =>` stands for the other cases)
kernel-nested-same = these arms match the same values
kernel-nested-lists-ctors = a kernel match lists constructors
kernel-pattern-unknown-implicit = `{ $name }` has no implicit argument named `{ $implicit }`
kernel-nested-field-numbers = `{ $name }` is written with different numbers of fields
kernel-nested-numeral-type = `{ $name }` is not a `Nat`: a numeral pattern is a natural number
kernel-nested-numeral-large = the numeral pattern `{ $numeral }` is larger than { $max }: it is read as { $numeral } nested `Succ`s; compare with `Nat::eq` instead

kernel-class-argument-arity = class argument count does not match its parameter telescope
kernel-instance-first-order = instance arguments must be first-order constructor heads, literals, or generics
kernel-instance-ambiguous = instance selection is ambiguous
kernel-instance-resolution-cycle = instance resolution contains a cyclic constraint
kernel-instance-resolution-depth = instance resolution exceeds the nesting limit
